1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,935
The brush clears a ring of standing stones with a heavy slab across them.

2
00:00:04,935 --> 00:00:07,307
This is not a house and not a temple.

3
00:00:11,433 --> 00:00:18,492
It is a grave — and it was built to be seen, by people who wanted the place where they buried their dead to be unmistakable on the landscape,

4
00:00:18,492 --> 00:00:22,023
a thousand years before anyone in the south wrote anything down.

5
00:00:27,833 --> 00:00:29,176
Only the ground again.

6
00:00:29,176 --> 00:00:34,313
These communities left no writing, and the Vedic texts of the north are not about them.

7
00:00:34,313 --> 00:00:39,099
Everything in this episode comes from excavation — mostly from excavating graves,

8
00:00:39,099 --> 00:00:43,076
which is a particular kind of evidence with a particular bias.

9
00:00:46,767 --> 00:00:55,658
From about 1200 BCE, across the Deccan and the far south, people began burying their dead under and inside large stone structures.

10
00:00:55,658 --> 00:00:58,102
Thousands of these sites are known.

11
00:00:58,102 --> 00:01:00,778
Brahmagiri and Maski in Karnataka.

12
00:01:03,433 --> 00:01:07,960
Hallur, where some of the earliest iron in the region has been reported.

13
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:11,990
Adichanallur in Tamil Nadu, with its great field of burial urns.

14
00:01:11,990 --> 00:01:14,399
Nagarjunakonda in Andhra Pradesh.

15
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:23,329
They are not all the same, and the names are worth knowing because examiners like them.

16
00:01:23,329 --> 00:01:26,539
A cist is a box of stone slabs sunk into the ground.

17
00:01:26,539 --> 00:01:30,412
A dolmen stands above ground, slabs supporting a capstone.

18
00:01:36,033 --> 00:01:39,496
A menhir is a single tall stone set upright as a marker.

19
00:01:39,496 --> 00:01:42,655
A cairn circle is a ring of stones around a burial.

20
00:01:42,655 --> 00:01:46,665
And an urn burial places the remains in a large pot in the earth.

21
00:01:46,665 --> 00:01:50,246
Which type you find depends largely on where you are.

22
00:01:57,333 --> 00:02:00,952
What went into the graves tells us about the living.

23
00:02:00,952 --> 00:02:07,874
Iron objects, in quantity: blades, spearheads, arrowheads, tools, sometimes fittings for horses.

24
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:16,302
And pottery of a distinctive kind, black on the inside and upper rim and red below,

25
00:02:16,302 --> 00:02:19,561
produced by the way the pots were stacked in the firing.

26
00:02:19,561 --> 00:02:24,377
Archaeologists call it Black and Red Ware, and it is the marker of this world.

27
00:02:28,367 --> 00:02:30,523
Two things make these cultures matter.

28
00:02:30,523 --> 00:02:30,910
First,

29
00:02:30,910 --> 00:02:35,775
iron appears in the south early — in some places as early as anywhere in the subcontine

30
00:02:35,775 --> 00:02:40,996
nt — which complicates any simple story of technology spreading outward from one centre.

31
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:51,371
Second, these communities were farming, herding and working metal in the very region where,

32
00:02:51,371 --> 00:02:55,867
a few centuries later, the Sangam poems describe chiefdoms, trade and towns.

33
00:02:55,867 --> 00:02:59,670
The megalith builders are the people underneath that world.

34
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:05,681
Are they the same people?

35
00:03:05,681 --> 00:03:11,046
Many scholars read a direct line from the megalithic communities to the chiefdoms of the Sangam age,

36
00:03:11,046 --> 00:03:14,798
pointing to continuity in sites, in pottery and in iron working.

37
00:03:18,967 --> 00:03:27,464
Others urge caution: there is a gap in the evidence, and continuity of objects is not the same as continuity of people or institutions.

38
00:03:27,464 --> 00:03:28,963
The link is reasonable.

39
00:03:28,963 --> 00:03:30,388
It is not proven.

40
00:03:34,533 --> 00:03:37,462
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

41
00:03:37,462 --> 00:03:38,961
Stone over the dead.

42
00:03:38,961 --> 00:03:40,547
Iron beside them.

43
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:47,096
Dig Quiz.

44
00:03:47,096 --> 00:03:49,244
Pause and name the burial.

45
00:03:49,244 --> 00:03:53,780
First: slabs standing above ground, carrying a capstone.

46
00:03:53,780 --> 00:03:54,876
A dolmen.

47
00:03:59,033 --> 00:04:03,427
Second: one tall stone set upright on its own, as a marker.

48
00:04:03,427 --> 00:04:04,159
A menhir.

49
00:04:04,159 --> 00:04:08,993
Third: the remains placed inside a large pot, set into the earth.

50
00:04:08,993 --> 00:04:10,391
An urn burial.

51
00:04:13,233 --> 00:04:19,317
In Mains, a useful question asks what megalithic burials tell us about society in the early south.

52
00:04:23,733 --> 00:04:29,610
Use the grave goods: iron in quantity, differences between graves pointing to differences in status,

53
00:04:29,610 --> 00:04:33,168
and a shared burial practice across a very wide region.

54
00:04:37,633 --> 00:04:42,331
A burial stone takes its place, and the second room of the gallery is full.

55
00:04:45,433 --> 00:04:51,246
Next gallery: back to the north, where sixteen states are about to appear and cities return.

56
00:04:51,246 --> 00:04:55,108
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

